{"file":{"content":"Here is the proof wish list I would use to prioritize the program. I am ordering these by **value toward Collatz**, not by ease.\n\n## 1. Quenched inverse theorem: bad orbit (\\Rightarrow) finite spectral shadow\n\nThis is the highest-value proof.\n\nThe desired statement is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{If a positive integer orbit sustains a bad deficit/large-deviation pattern,}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{it casts a non-coboundary finite spectral shadow.}\n}\n]\n\nMore explicitly, if an orbit has infinitely many long windows where\n\n[\nD_{n+\\ell}-D_n-\\ell\\log_2 3\n]\n\nfails the expected bounded/negative-drift behavior in the way required for a counterexample, then there should exist finite parameters\n\n[\nQ=3^r,\\qquad L,\\qquad \\chi\\neq 1,\\qquad s\\in[0,1],\n]\n\nwith (\\chi) non-coboundary, such that the normalized defect statistic\n\n[\n\\mathcal S_{n,\\ell}(Q,\\chi,s)\n]\n\nremains persistently large.\n\nThis is the true moon. It is also the hardest. It is the precise deterministic-equidistribution theorem your whole program keeps reducing to.\n\nWhat might work: do not try to prove full normality. Prove a **failure-implies-structure** theorem. The strongest route is an inverse theorem:\n\n[\n\\text{persistent large-deviation renewal}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{finite character/coboundary obstruction}.\n]\n\nThis is analogous in shape to Green--Tao inverse theory, but for the residue-height affine cocycle. The recent defect-cocycle work gives the right finite detector; now the theorem must show that every self-sustaining counterexample must trigger it.\n\n\n\nThis is probably new mathematics.\n\n---\n\n## 2. No further coboundaries beyond the quadratic valuation-parity sector\n\nThis is the most important **finite** proof.\n\nYou have already shown the quadratic character\n\n[\n\\chi_2\n]\n\nis not a true obstruction because\n\n[\n\\chi_2(S(x))=(-1)^{v_2(3x+1)}.\n]\n\nSo H23a must be stated on the quotient by this exact coboundary. The next proof is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\operatorname{Cob}(\\mathcal L^{\\mathrm{sync}})=\\langle \\chi_2\\rangle.\n}\n]\n\nEquivalently: no principal-unit character modulo (3^r) is an exact synchronized coboundary.\n\nThe promising reduction is to a finite (3)-adic algebra lemma. If the principal-unit character is (\\psi), prove that the functional equation\n\n[\n\\psi(3a+1)=\\psi(a^2)\n]\n\non\n\n[\nC=1+3\\mathbb Z/3^r\\mathbb Z\n]\n\nforces (\\psi=1).\n\nUse the (3)-adic logarithm:\n\n[\n\\log:1+3\\mathbb Z/3^r\\mathbb Z\\to 3\\mathbb Z/3^r\\mathbb Z.\n]\n\nThen show the subgroup generated by\n\n[\n\\log(3a+1)-2\\log(a)\n]\n\nas (a) varies over principal units is the full additive principal-unit group. Any character vanishing on that subgroup is trivial.\n\nThis proof feels accessible. It is finite, local, and algebraic. I would attack this first.\n\n\n\nThis is the best next target.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Sync quotient gap: (\\kappa_Q\\gg 1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q))\n\nThis is the quantitative form of H23a after the coboundary correction.\n\nThe theorem:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\rho(\\mathcal L^{\\mathrm{sync}}*{s,Q,\\chi})\n\t\\le\n\t(1-\\kappa_Q)\\rho(\\mathcal L^{\\mathrm{sync}}*{s,Q,0})\n}\n]\n\nfor every non-coboundary character (\\chi), uniformly for (s\\in[0,1]), with\n\n[\n\\kappa_Q\\gg \\frac{1}{(\\log Q)^A}.\n]\n\nNumerically, the gap appears much stronger:\n\n[\n\\kappa_Q\\approx 0.093\n]\n\nand flat through tested (Q=3^5). But first prove the polylog version. Constant gap can be a later strengthening.\n\nWhat might work: principal-unit cancellation.\n\nFor every non-coboundary character (\\chi), find a short block family (\\mathcal W) of synchronized valuation blocks whose endpoint residues contain enough variation in a subgroup (H\\subset U_Q) on which (\\chi) is nontrivial. Then use character orthogonality:\n\n[\n\\sum_{u\\in H}\\chi(uh)=0.\n]\n\nThe proof should be a block Doeblin/minorization argument:\n\n[\n\\text{accessible block mass}\\ge \\alpha_Q,\n\\qquad\n\\text{phase cancellation}\\ge \\eta_Q,\n]\n\nso\n\n[\n\\kappa_Q\\gg \\alpha_Q\\eta_Q.\n]\n\nTry to build block families with identical valuation multisets so the height weights match exactly. That avoids distortion when passing from residue-only to synchronized residue-height operators.\n\n\n\nThis is likely the most valuable analytic proof after no-further-coboundaries.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Conductor monotonicity or conductor-(9) extremality\n\nThis is the stronger version of the previous theorem.\n\nNumerically, the worst non-coboundary sector seems to occur at low conductor, probably conductor (9). The desired theorem is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\sup_{\\chi\\text{ non-coboundary mod }3^r}\n\t\\frac{\\rho(\\mathcal L_{\\chi})}{\\rho(\\mathcal L_0)}\n\t==================================================\n\t\n\t\\sup_{\\chi\\text{ conductor }9}\n\t\\frac{\\rho(\\mathcal L_{\\chi})}{\\rho(\\mathcal L_0)}.\n}\n]\n\nIf true, then\n\n[\n\\kappa_Q\\ge \\kappa_0>0\n]\n\nuniformly in (Q).\n\nWhat might work: show high-conductor principal-unit characters are killed by complete-fiber averaging. High-conductor characters should oscillate faster, not slower. If the transition images contain enough full fibers modulo (3^k), then high conductor cancels more strongly.\n\nThe proof shape:\n\n1. decompose\n\n[\nU_{3^r}\\cong {\\pm1}\\times (1+3\\mathbb Z/3^r\\mathbb Z);\n]\n\n2. quotient ({\\pm1}) by the quadratic coboundary;\n\n3. show the principal-unit transition kernel is smoothing in conductor;\n\n4. prove conductor cannot increase the spectral ratio.\n\n\n\nThis may be harder than the polylog gap but would produce a much cleaner theorem.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Height/killing distortion transfer\n\nOnce the residue gap is proved, one must show it survives the full synchronized residue-height operator.\n\nThe theorem:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{principal-unit cancellation persists after height weights and killing.}\n}\n]\n\nThis means that if a block family cancels characters in residue space, then the weighted block family also cancels after multiplying by\n\n[\n2^{s\\sum_i(\\log_2 3-b_i)}\n]\n\nand restricting to the killed strip.\n\nThe easiest way is to design cancelling blocks with the same valuation multiset:\n\n[\n{b_i}*{w_1}={b_i}*{w_2}.\n]\n\nThen their height weights are identical:\n\n[\nw_s(w_1)=w_s(w_2).\n]\n\nIf identical multisets are unavailable, prove bounded distortion:\n\n[\nC^{-1}\\le\\frac{w_s(w_1)}{w_s(w_2)}\\le C,\n\\qquad 0\\le s\\le1.\n]\n\nFor killing, prove the cancellation blocks remain in the QSD bulk except for controlled boundary loss:\n\n[\nO(e^{-cL})\n]\n\nor a polylog-compatible error.\n\n\n\nThis is probably bookkeeping once the residue cancellation blocks are explicit.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Critical-bridge non-concatenation / post-exit taboo theorem\n\nYour computations strongly suggest:\n\n[\n\\text{critical excursion}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{post-exit anti-tilt}.\n]\n\nA strong theorem would say:\n\n[\nE(C_6)\\cap C_6=\\varnothing,\n]\n\nor more realistically:\n\n[\n\\rho(K_6 E K_6)<1,\n]\n\nwhere (K_6) is the critical-excursion operator and (E) is the post-exit operator.\n\nIn entropy language:\n\n[\n\\text{a Cramér-tilted bridge cannot concatenate with another Cramér-tilted bridge.}\n]\n\nWhat might work: prove that after a high ascent governed by\n\n[\np_b^{(1)}=3\\cdot4^{-b},\n]\n\nthe exit affine correction forces the next launch law away from (p^{(1)}). Quantify this by showing\n\n[\nM_{\\mathrm{post}}(1)<1-\\epsilon\n]\n\nuniformly, not just empirically.\n\nThis could give an internal route to cycle elimination or at least to Exit.\n\n\n\nThis is potentially powerful but less local than the finite-operator tasks.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Height-tail theorem for (C_A)\n\nProve rigorously:\n\n[\nC_A={V:h(V)\\ge\\log_2V-A}\n]\n\nhas\n\n[\n#(C_A\\cap[2^N,2^{N+1}))=O_A(1)\n]\n\nor at least\n\n[\nO_A(N^B).\n]\n\nThe annealed reason is the Cramér root:\n\n[\nM(1)=1\n]\n\nand the tail law\n\n[\n\\Pr(h\\ge H)\\asymp 2^{-H}.\n]\n\nWhat might work: a renewal theorem for the forward height cocycle plus deterministic counting of finite valuation words. Since finite words are broadly realizable, this might reduce to counting high-excursion words with exact congruence realizability.\n\nBut be careful: a purely annealed proof only gives the model. You need an integer-counting theorem, not just a probability estimate.\n\n\n\nUseful for Exit; not sufficient for Collatz by itself.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Finite-word realizability with uniform fiber control\n\nMany reductions need a theorem of the form:\n\nFor a valuation word\n\n[\nw=(b_0,\\dots,b_{m-1}),\n]\n\nthe set of integers realizing (w) is a complete residue class modulo\n\n[\n2^{B_m},\n\\qquad B_m=\\sum_i b_i,\n]\n\nand the residue distribution modulo (3^r) is controlled uniformly in (m,r).\n\nThe exact congruence is already known in pieces. The high-value theorem is a uniform version:\n\n[\n\\text{complete fibers with distortion }\\theta_Q\\text{ controlled as a function of }Q.\n]\n\nThis was formerly H23-like. It remains useful because it supports the passage from word-level annealed counts to exact residue-height operators.\n\nWhat might work: explicit affine formula\n\n[\nx_m=\\frac{3^m x_0+A_m}{2^{B_m}},\n]\n\nsolve congruences modulo (2^{B_m}), then track induced residues modulo (3^r) using the sliding-window identity.\n\n\n\nThis is not glamorous, but it can remove technical uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Normalized spectral-shadow calibration theorem\n\nThe raw defect cocycle\n\n[\nW_{n,\\ell}(x;\\chi,s)\n]\n\nis contaminated by small-(Q) inflation. You refined the object to a normalized spectral shadow\n\n[\n\\mathcal S_{n,\\ell}(Q,\\chi,s)\n=============================\n\n\\frac{|W_{n,\\ell}|}{\\mathrm{Null}_{Q,\\chi,s}(\\ell)}.\n]\n\nA useful theorem would be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Under the annealed/sync-null model, }\n\t\\mathcal S_{n,\\ell}\n\t\\text{ has a calibrated tail bound uniformly in }Q,\\chi,s.\n}\n]\n\nThis would distinguish real shadows from finite-window artifacts.\n\nWhat might work: use the finite sync operator gap to get concentration:\n\n[\n\\mathbb P(|W_{n,\\ell}|>\\epsilon)\n\\le\nC\\exp(-c\\epsilon^2\\ell)\n]\n\nwith constants depending on the quotient gap.\n\nThis would not prove Collatz, but it would make the computational detector mathematically honest.\n\n\n\nImportant for numerical interpretation.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Cycle elimination, Route A: moving-strip SRCE\n\nThe fixed-strip SRCE machinery does not automatically eliminate cycles because a cycle lives in a moving strip. The desired theorem is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{moving-strip survival still forces a finite spectral shadow or descent.}\n}\n]\n\nThis would internalize cycle elimination into the same residue-height framework.\n\nWhat might work: combine post-exit taboo with the sync quotient gap. A cycle would need infinitely repeated critical renewals. If every renewal is anti-tilted or casts a forbidden finite shadow, then cycles are ruled out.\n\nBut this is later-stage. First prove the finite operator gap.\n\n\n\nPotentially decisive, but not the next proof to attempt.\n\n---\n\n# My recommended proof order\n\nI would proceed in this exact order.\n\n## Phase 1: Finish the finite operator theorem\n\n1. Prove no further coboundaries:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Cob}=\\langle\\chi_2\\rangle.\n]\n\n2. Prove principal-unit cancellation:\n\n[\n\\kappa_Q\\gg1/\\operatorname{polylog}(Q).\n]\n\n3. Transfer the residue gap to the synchronized residue-height killed operator.\n\nThis would complete the corrected H23a operator side.\n\n## Phase 2: Turn finite gaps into calibrated shadows\n\n4. Define and prove tail bounds for normalized spectral shadows.\n\n5. Show critical windows/post-exit windows behave according to the calibrated theory.\n\nThis makes the defect cocycle analytically reliable.\n\n## Phase 3: Attack the true quenched inverse arrow\n\n6. Prove:\n\n[\n\\text{self-sustaining bad orbit}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{persistent normalized finite spectral shadow}.\n]\n\nThis is the real new theorem. Do it only after the finite target is fully proved.\n\n## Phase 4: Cycle elimination / moving strip\n\n7. Use the finite-shadow theorem plus post-exit taboo to rule out self-renewing cycles.\n\n---\n\n# What I think is most likely to work\n\nThe most likely near-term proof is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\operatorname{Cob}=\\langle\\chi_2\\rangle.\n}\n]\n\nIt is finite (3)-adic algebra and should be attacked now.\n\nThe second most likely is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\kappa_Q\\gg1/\\operatorname{polylog}(Q)\n}\n]\n\nvia principal-unit block cancellation.\n\nThe long-term Collatz-critical theorem is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{bad orbit}\\Rightarrow\\text{finite spectral shadow}.\n}\n]\n\nThat is the deepest and least standard. But your recent work has at least made the target finite and precise.\n\nSo the proof wish list, in one line, is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{No more coboundaries}\n\t;\\Rightarrow;\n\t\\text{principal-unit quotient gap}\n\t;\\Rightarrow;\n\t\\text{height-stable finite spectral shadow}\n\t;\\Rightarrow;\n\t\\text{bad orbit casts a shadow}\n\t;\\Rightarrow;\n\t\\text{Collatz modulo cycle elimination}.\n}\n]\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_proofs.md","numLines":574,"startLine":1,"totalLines":574},"type":"text"}